    "*[AI-assisted: START]*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q16. Which similarity metric performed best, and does it match Section 3.2?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Baselines (computed in Part E): predicting the global mean gives RMSE 1.050 and predicting each user's own mean gives 0.952. All numbers below are from the main grid: neighbors need at least 2 co-rated entries and the prediction is the raw weighted average of the neighbors' ratings (Section 3.5).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Best configuration.** The best configuration is item-based Euclidean with k=50 (RMSE 1.045, but 38% of the test ratings could not be predicted). For user-based the best is cosine with k=50 (1.156, 56% None). Pearson is the worst for item-based (1.24 to 1.27) and also at small k for user-based (1.37 at k=5), and no configuration beats the user-mean baseline of 0.952. This is the opposite of what Section 3.2 suggests, where Pearson should be the most reliable.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why.** Section 3.2 says Pearson is good at *choosing* neighbors with the same taste regardless of how harsh or generous they are. But the prediction in Section 3.5 averages the neighbors' *raw* ratings, so a harsh neighbor with Pearson close to 1 still pulls the prediction down; Euclidean picks neighbors whose absolute ratings are close, which suits a raw average. Also, Pearson on 2 or 3 co-rated entries is almost always plus or minus 1 (Parts C and D show many similarities of exactly 1.0), so many \"perfect\" neighbors are just noise.\n",
    "\n",
    "**k and coverage.** RMSE mostly falls as k grows (k=50 is best in five of six cases, so the optimum may be higher): small k is high variance, while a large k adds less similar neighbors. The exception is item-based Pearson, which is best at k=10 and gets worse after that. The fraction of None falls as k grows (user-based Pearson goes from 91% to 50%) because more neighbors means more chances that one of them rated the target, so the RMSEs of different rows cover different subsets of the test set.\n",
    "\n",
