    "Euclidean ranks A above B for T; Pearson ranks B above A. A is the wrong neighbour (its pattern is the reverse of T's, but its raw numbers are close), while B is a harsh rater with exactly T's taste. The pair T and A alone is the two-user version (0.333 vs -1); B is added so that a ranking exists.\n",
